var e=[{id:`PP12-0001`,q:`Which gas present in engine exhaust poses the greatest hazard to occupants if exhaust leaks enter a cabin heating system?`,a:[`Carbon monoxide.`,`Carbon dioxide.`,`Oxygen.`],c:0,exp:`Carbon monoxide is a product of incomplete combustion present in exhaust gas. If a cracked exhaust shroud allows it into the cabin heat, it can incapacitate or kill occupants.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0002`,q:`Which statement best describes the physical properties of carbon monoxide?`,a:[`It has a sharp acrid odor that quickly warns occupants.`,`It is a colorless, odorless, and tasteless gas.`,`It is a visible bluish-green gas that is easy to detect.`],c:1,exp:`Carbon monoxide is colorless, odorless, and tasteless, so occupants receive no natural warning of its presence. This is why exhaust system integrity and CO detectors are critical.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0003`,q:`On many light reciprocating-engine aircraft, cabin heat is most commonly obtained by`,a:[`routing engine coolant through a cabin radiator.`,`using only an electric resistance heating element.`,`passing fresh ram air over a shroud or muff surrounding the exhaust.`],c:2,exp:`Cabin heat on most light aircraft is produced by directing fresh air over a heat exchanger (shroud or muff) on the exhaust system. A crack in that exhaust component can introduce carbon monoxide into the cabin.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0004`,q:`What is the primary purpose of the exhaust collector ring on a radial engine?`,a:[`To collect exhaust from the individual cylinders and route it to a common outlet or turbosupercharger.`,`To cool the cylinders by directing air over the fins.`,`To store exhaust gas to reduce engine vibration.`],c:0,exp:`A collector ring gathers exhaust from each cylinder into a single duct that leads to the tailpipe or turbosupercharger. It allows the exhaust to be discharged at one point rather than from many separate stacks.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0005`,q:`Why are slip joints incorporated into many reciprocating-engine exhaust systems?`,a:[`To allow drainage of unburned fuel from the system.`,`To permit thermal expansion and contraction without cracking.`,`To increase exhaust gas velocity at the outlet.`],c:1,exp:`Slip joints allow sections of the exhaust system to expand and contract with temperature changes. Without them, the heating and cooling cycles would crack the rigidly joined components.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0006`,q:`During inspection of a reciprocating-engine exhaust system, cracks are most frequently found`,a:[`in the center of straight tubing sections away from joints.`,`only on the internal baffle surfaces.`,`at welds, clamps, slip joints, and points of stress concentration.`],c:2,exp:`Cracks usually develop at welds, clamps, slip joints, and other areas of stress concentration where heating, vibration, and fatigue act together. These areas should be examined carefully during inspection.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0007`,q:`A gray or sooty streak trailing from a joint in an exhaust system most likely indicates`,a:[`an exhaust gas leak at that joint.`,`normal heat discoloration of the metal.`,`correct slip-joint lubrication.`],c:0,exp:`Light gray or sooty streaks emanating from an exhaust joint are a classic sign of a gas leak. The escaping hot gas deposits residue and may signal a crack or loose connection requiring repair.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0008`,q:`Which materials are commonly used in the construction of reciprocating-engine exhaust systems because of their heat and corrosion resistance?`,a:[`Aluminum alloy and magnesium.`,`Stainless steel and nickel-based alloys.`,`Copper and brass.`],c:1,exp:`Exhaust components are typically made of stainless steel or nickel-based alloys (such as Inconel) that resist the high temperatures and corrosive gases. Lighter metals like aluminum or magnesium would not survive the heat.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0009`,q:`Why should an exhaust system component never be marked with an ordinary lead pencil prior to installation?`,a:[`The mark cannot be seen at operating temperature.`,`The mark increases exhaust back pressure.`,`Carbon from the pencil can cause cracking of the heated metal.`],c:2,exp:`Carbon deposited by a lead pencil can be absorbed into the hot metal and create a stress point that leads to cracking. Approved marking methods should be used instead.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0010`,q:`What is the function of the heat exchanger (heat muff) installed around an exhaust stack?`,a:[`To transfer exhaust heat to ventilating air for cabin or carburetor heat.`,`To increase exhaust gas velocity for thrust.`,`To muffle exhaust noise from the cylinders.`],c:0,exp:`A heat muff is a shroud around the exhaust that allows ventilating air to pick up heat for cabin heating or carburetor heat. Its integrity is critical to prevent carbon monoxide from entering the airflow.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0011`,q:`A reliable method for detecting small leaks in an exhaust heat exchanger used for cabin heat is to`,a:[`feel the surface for warmth with the engine running.`,`pressurize the system and check for leaks with the engine off.`,`listen for a whistling sound during taxi only.`],c:1,exp:`Pressure-testing the exhaust heat exchanger and checking for escaping air or pressure loss reveals cracks that could admit carbon monoxide into the cabin air. This is a recommended inspection technique for these components.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0012`,q:`Which symptoms are commonly associated with carbon monoxide poisoning of a pilot or crew?`,a:[`Sharpened vision and increased alertness.`,`Loud ringing in the ears with no other effect.`,`Headache, drowsiness, dizziness, and impaired judgment.`],c:2,exp:`Carbon monoxide reduces the blood's ability to carry oxygen, producing headache, drowsiness, dizziness, and impaired judgment. Because the gas is undetectable by the senses, these symptoms may be the first warning.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0013`,q:`When reassembling an exhaust system joint that uses a gasket, the proper practice is to`,a:[`install a new gasket of the correct type.`,`reuse the original gasket to maintain a proven seal.`,`omit the gasket and rely on metal-to-metal contact.`],c:0,exp:`Exhaust gaskets should be replaced with new gaskets of the correct type whenever a joint is opened. A used gasket may have taken a set or been damaged and can allow a hazardous gas leak.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0014`,q:`What may result from warping or distortion of an exhaust stack mounting flange?`,a:[`Improved sealing against the cylinder port.`,`A gas leak at the flange and possible carbon monoxide hazard.`,`Reduced exhaust gas temperature at the cylinder.`],c:1,exp:`A warped flange cannot seal evenly against the cylinder exhaust port, allowing hot gas to escape. Such a leak can damage surrounding structure and introduce carbon monoxide into heated air.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0015`,q:`When tightening exhaust system clamps and nuts, the mechanic should`,a:[`torque them as tightly as possible to prevent any leak.`,`leave them finger-tight to allow thermal movement.`,`tighten to the specified torque to avoid distortion and cracking.`],c:2,exp:`Exhaust fasteners must be tightened to the manufacturer's specified torque. Overtightening can distort or crack the thin-walled components, while undertightening allows leaks.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0016`,q:`The short-stack (open) exhaust system, in which each cylinder discharges through its own short stack, is generally used on`,a:[`low-power, non-supercharged engines where back pressure must be minimal.`,`high-altitude turbosupercharged transport engines.`,`engines requiring maximum cabin heat output.`],c:0,exp:`Short individual stacks are simple and impose little back pressure, making them suitable for low-power, non-supercharged engines. They provide little noise suppression and limited heat recovery.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0017`,q:`Internal failure of a baffle or cone inside an exhaust muffler can be especially dangerous because it may`,a:[`increase the octane requirement of the fuel.`,`restrict the exhaust outlet and cause a loss of engine power.`,`improve scavenging and increase power output.`],c:1,exp:`A loose internal baffle can shift and partially block the exhaust outlet, creating excessive back pressure and a serious loss of engine power. This internal damage may not be visible externally.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0018`,q:`An exhaust augmentor system on a reciprocating engine functions primarily to`,a:[`store exhaust gases to smooth out pressure pulses.`,`convert exhaust heat directly into electrical power.`,`use exhaust flow to create low pressure that draws additional cooling air through the engine.`],c:2,exp:`Augmentor tubes use the velocity of exhaust gas to create a low-pressure region that pulls extra cooling air across the engine and may provide a small amount of added thrust. This aids cooling, especially at low airspeed.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0019`,q:`A nondestructive method well suited for detecting surface cracks in nonmagnetic stainless steel exhaust components is`,a:[`dye penetrant inspection.`,`magnetic particle inspection.`,`eddy current of internal welds only.`],c:0,exp:`Dye penetrant inspection works on nonmagnetic materials such as stainless steel exhaust parts and reveals surface cracks. Magnetic particle inspection is limited to ferromagnetic materials.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0020`,q:`Before an exhaust system is inspected for cracks and leaks, the components should first be`,a:[`painted to highlight surface defects.`,`cleaned of soot and carbon deposits.`,`coated with grease to seal small cracks.`],c:1,exp:`Soot and carbon must be removed so that cracks, leaks, and hot spots become visible during inspection. Deposits can hide defects that would otherwise be found.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0021`,q:`On a turbosupercharged reciprocating engine, the exhaust gas is used to`,a:[`preheat the incoming fuel before injection.`,`lubricate the turbocharger bearings.`,`drive the turbine wheel of the turbosupercharger.`],c:2,exp:`In a turbosupercharged installation, exhaust gas is routed to drive the turbine, which spins the compressor to boost induction air pressure. The exhaust system therefore carries high-pressure, high-temperature gas and must be leak-free.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0022`,q:`A waste gate in a turbosupercharger exhaust system controls boost by`,a:[`varying the amount of exhaust gas bypassed around the turbine.`,`regulating fuel flow to the cylinders.`,`changing the pitch of the propeller.`],c:0,exp:`The waste gate regulates how much exhaust gas flows through the turbine versus bypassing it, thereby controlling turbocharger speed and manifold pressure. Its housing is part of the exhaust system and must be inspected for leaks and cracks.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0023`,q:`Localized bluish or discolored areas combined with a buildup of light-colored deposits on an exhaust stack typically indicate`,a:[`a normal break-in condition requiring no action.`,`a hot spot or leak that should be investigated.`,`excessive oil consumption from the cylinders.`],c:1,exp:`Such discoloration and deposits often mark a hot spot or leaking joint where hot gas escapes. The area should be cleaned and inspected closely for cracks.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0024`,q:`If a cabin heater muff exhaust component is found cracked during inspection, the proper action is to`,a:[`seal the crack with high-temperature tape and return to service.`,`reduce cabin heat use but continue flying indefinitely.`,`repair or replace the component before further flight.`],c:2,exp:`A cracked heater exhaust component must be repaired or replaced before flight because it can admit carbon monoxide into the cabin air. Temporary patches are not acceptable for this safety-critical part.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0025`,q:`Which inspection practice helps reveal cracks at exhaust slip joints that may be hidden when assembled?`,a:[`Disassembling and inspecting the joint and overlapping surfaces.`,`Increasing engine RPM and listening for noise only.`,`Measuring exhaust gas temperature at cruise only.`],c:0,exp:`Cracks at slip joints are often concealed by the overlapping sections, so disassembly and inspection of the mating surfaces is needed to find them. Visual checks of the assembled joint alone can miss these defects.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K1`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0026`,q:`In a turbine engine, the primary purpose of the exhaust cone (tail cone) assembly is to`,a:[`increase the temperature of the gases entering the turbine.`,`straighten and smooth the gas flow leaving the turbine and convert some velocity to pressure.`,`compress the incoming air before combustion.`],c:1,exp:`The exhaust cone collects the swirling gases leaving the turbine and straightens the flow, reducing turbulence. Its diverging passage slows the gas slightly and raises static pressure to improve efficiency.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0027`,q:`The radial struts located in a turbine engine exhaust cone assembly serve to`,a:[`spray fuel into the exhaust stream.`,`generate electrical power from the exhaust heat.`,`support the inner cone and straighten the exhaust gas flow.`],c:2,exp:`The struts support the inner exhaust cone and help straighten the gas flow as it exits the turbine. Removing swirl improves exhaust efficiency and thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0028`,q:`A convergent exhaust nozzle on a turbine engine affects the exhaust gas by`,a:[`increasing gas velocity and decreasing pressure as the area narrows.`,`decreasing gas velocity and increasing pressure.`,`having no effect on velocity or pressure.`],c:0,exp:`A convergent nozzle has a decreasing cross-sectional area, which accelerates the subsonic exhaust gas and lowers its static pressure. This increase in velocity helps produce thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0029`,q:`A convergent-divergent (C-D) exhaust nozzle is used primarily on engines designed to operate at`,a:[`very low subsonic cruise speeds.`,`supersonic exhaust gas velocities.`,`idle power only.`],c:1,exp:`A convergent-divergent nozzle is used where exhaust gas reaches supersonic velocity, allowing further expansion and acceleration in the divergent section. This design recovers thrust that a simple convergent nozzle could not.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0030`,q:`In the divergent section of a convergent-divergent nozzle, supersonic exhaust gas`,a:[`decelerates and stops flowing.`,`reverses direction toward the turbine.`,`continues to accelerate as the area increases.`],c:2,exp:`Once the gas is supersonic, increasing the area in the divergent section allows it to expand and accelerate further. This behavior is opposite to that of subsonic flow in a diverging duct.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0031`,q:`The tailpipe of a turbine engine exhaust system primarily`,a:[`ducts the exhaust gases from the engine to the nozzle and atmosphere.`,`mixes fuel with the exhaust gas.`,`drives the accessory gearbox.`],c:0,exp:`The tailpipe carries the exhaust gases from the rear of the engine to the exhaust nozzle and out to the atmosphere. It must withstand high temperatures and be inspected for cracks and warping.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0032`,q:`Turbine engine exhaust sections are typically constructed of which type of material?`,a:[`Aluminum alloy.`,`Heat-resistant nickel or stainless steel alloys.`,`Reinforced fiberglass.`],c:1,exp:`Exhaust sections operate at very high temperatures and are made from heat-resistant nickel-based or stainless steel alloys. Lower-melting metals such as aluminum could not survive the heat.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0033`,q:`During inspection of a turbine engine exhaust section, localized discolored areas that are darker or differently colored than the surrounding metal usually indicate`,a:[`normal surface oxidation requiring no concern.`,`areas of recent welding repair.`,`hot spots caused by uneven temperature, such as a malfunctioning fuel nozzle.`],c:2,exp:`Hot spots appear as discolored areas and often point to upstream problems such as a faulty fuel nozzle or combustion irregularity. They warrant investigation of both the exhaust and combustion sections.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0034`,q:`Cracks found in a turbine engine exhaust cone or tailpipe are most commonly the result of`,a:[`thermal stress and vibration concentrated at welds and stiffeners.`,`corrosion from fuel additives only.`,`foreign object damage from the inlet.`],c:0,exp:`Repeated heating and cooling combined with vibration concentrate stress at welds, stiffeners, and changes in section, producing cracks. These areas should receive close attention during inspection.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0035`,q:`Which inspection method is commonly used to detect surface cracks in turbine exhaust components made of nonmagnetic alloys?`,a:[`Magnetic particle inspection.`,`Fluorescent or dye penetrant inspection.`,`Hardness testing.`],c:1,exp:`Because exhaust components are often nonmagnetic, penetrant inspection (dye or fluorescent) is used to reveal surface cracks. Magnetic particle methods only work on ferromagnetic materials.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0036`,q:`The exhaust gas temperature (EGT) thermocouples in a turbine engine are located to sense temperature`,a:[`at the compressor inlet.`,`in the oil tank.`,`in the turbine or exhaust gas stream.`],c:2,exp:`EGT thermocouples are placed in the turbine or exhaust gas path to indicate the temperature of the gases, which reflects engine operating condition. Excessive readings can indicate combustion or turbine problems.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0037`,q:`Why must the exhaust nozzle area (effective exhaust opening) of a turbine engine be maintained within limits?`,a:[`An incorrect nozzle area changes exhaust velocity and affects EGT and thrust.`,`It has no measurable effect on engine performance.`,`It only affects engine noise, not performance.`],c:0,exp:`The nozzle area sets the exhaust gas velocity and back pressure, which directly affect EGT, RPM, and thrust. An incorrect area can cause the engine to operate outside its limits.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0038`,q:`In a turbofan engine, the cold (fan) stream and hot (core) exhaust may be discharged through`,a:[`a single duct only with no separation.`,`either separate nozzles or a common mixed-flow nozzle, depending on design.`,`the engine inlet.`],c:1,exp:`Turbofan designs may exhaust the fan and core streams through separate nozzles or mix them in a common nozzle. The arrangement affects efficiency, thrust, and noise characteristics.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0039`,q:`The thrust produced by a turbojet engine exhaust is greatest when the exhaust gases leave the nozzle at`,a:[`the lowest possible velocity.`,`zero velocity.`,`a high velocity relative to the aircraft.`],c:2,exp:`Thrust depends on accelerating the gas to a high velocity as it exits the nozzle. The reaction to this high-velocity discharge produces forward thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0040`,q:`As exhaust gas flows through a fixed-area convergent nozzle and the engine reaches a condition where the nozzle is choked, the gas velocity at the nozzle throat is limited to`,a:[`the speed of sound (Mach 1).`,`twice the speed of sound.`,`the aircraft's airspeed.`],c:0,exp:`A choked convergent nozzle reaches sonic velocity (Mach 1) at the throat and cannot accelerate the subsonic flow further. Additional expansion to supersonic speed requires a divergent section.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0041`,q:`Warping or buckling of a turbine engine tailpipe is most likely caused by`,a:[`normal cold-weather operation.`,`exposure to excessive or uneven exhaust temperatures.`,`use of approved cleaning solvents.`],c:1,exp:`Excessive or uneven heating distorts the thin exhaust metal, producing warping or buckling. Such distortion may indicate an overtemperature condition and requires inspection and possible replacement.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0042`,q:`When inspecting turbine exhaust components, the term "hot streak" or "hot spot" most directly suggests a problem in the`,a:[`lubrication system.`,`landing gear actuation system.`,`combustion or fuel-nozzle area upstream of the exhaust.`],c:2,exp:`A hot spot in the exhaust usually traces back to uneven combustion or a malfunctioning fuel nozzle upstream. The exhaust discoloration is a symptom that calls for inspecting the combustion section.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0043`,q:`The insulation blanket sometimes installed around a turbine engine tailpipe is used primarily to`,a:[`reduce heat transfer to surrounding structure and lower fire risk.`,`increase exhaust gas velocity.`,`add weight for balance.`],c:0,exp:`An insulation blanket limits heat radiated from the hot tailpipe to surrounding structure, reducing the fire hazard and protecting nearby components. It must be properly secured and maintained.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0044`,q:`Compared with a reciprocating engine exhaust, the gases leaving a turbine engine exhaust nozzle are characterized by`,a:[`lower temperature and very low velocity.`,`high velocity and high temperature used to produce thrust.`,`no measurable velocity at all.`],c:1,exp:`Turbine exhaust gas exits at high velocity and high temperature, and this momentum produces thrust. The exhaust system is therefore an integral part of thrust production, not merely a means of discharge.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0045`,q:`Which condition would most likely cause an increase in exhaust gas temperature reading for a given power setting on a turbine engine?`,a:[`A clean, undamaged turbine and exhaust section.`,`Operation at a colder ambient temperature.`,`Turbine or combustion section deterioration restricting efficient operation.`],c:2,exp:`Deterioration of the turbine or combustion section reduces efficiency and raises the EGT needed to produce a given thrust. Monitoring EGT trends helps detect such internal engine problems.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0046`,q:`Why is the inner surface of a turbine exhaust cone inspected for cracks and burning?`,a:[`Because it is exposed to high-temperature gas and can fail, disrupting flow.`,`Because it carries fuel to the burners.`,`Because it supports the main engine bearings.`],c:0,exp:`The exhaust cone is directly exposed to hot, high-velocity gas and can develop cracks or burned areas. Failure could disrupt exhaust flow and release pieces downstream, so it is inspected carefully.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0047`,q:`In a turbine engine with a fixed-area exhaust nozzle, the exhaust gas pressure at the nozzle, relative to the pressure at the turbine outlet, is`,a:[`higher than turbine outlet pressure.`,`lower, because the converging nozzle accelerates the gas.`,`exactly equal at all power settings.`],c:1,exp:`As gas accelerates through the converging nozzle, its static pressure drops below the turbine outlet pressure while velocity increases. This conversion of pressure to velocity is what generates thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0048`,q:`Foreign object debris or pieces found in the tailpipe during inspection should be`,a:[`ignored if the engine runs smoothly.`,`pushed back into the engine for combustion.`,`investigated to determine the source, as they may indicate internal engine damage.`],c:2,exp:`Metal pieces or debris in the tailpipe can indicate failure of internal turbine or combustion components. The source must be identified before the engine is returned to service.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0049`,q:`The exhaust gas leaving the turbine of a turbojet still contains usable energy that the exhaust nozzle converts into`,a:[`high-velocity gas flow for thrust.`,`electrical current.`,`additional fuel.`],c:0,exp:`After driving the turbine, the gas retains pressure and heat energy, which the nozzle converts into a high-velocity jet for thrust. Efficient nozzle design maximizes this thrust contribution.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0050`,q:`Some turbine engines use a variable-area exhaust nozzle to`,a:[`change the color of the exhaust.`,`optimize exhaust gas flow for different operating conditions such as afterburner use.`,`store exhaust gas during shutdown.`],c:1,exp:`A variable-area nozzle adjusts the exhaust opening to match different operating conditions, such as when an afterburner is engaged. This maintains proper engine operation across a wide thrust range.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K2`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0051`,q:`The primary purpose of a muffler or noise suppressor on an engine exhaust system is to`,a:[`increase engine power output.`,`raise the exhaust gas temperature.`,`reduce the sound energy of the exhaust noise.`],c:2,exp:`A muffler or suppressor reduces the noise energy produced by the exhaust. It accomplishes this without unduly restricting the exhaust flow.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0052`,q:`Much of the noise produced by a turbojet exhaust is generated by`,a:[`the violent mixing of the high-velocity exhaust jet with the surrounding still air.`,`the smooth, low-velocity flow at idle.`,`the compressor inlet only.`],c:0,exp:`Jet exhaust noise comes largely from the turbulent shearing and mixing of the high-velocity exhaust with the relatively still ambient air. Reducing the velocity difference or promoting gentler mixing lowers the noise.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0053`,q:`A multi-tube or multi-lobe (corrugated-perimeter) jet exhaust noise suppressor reduces noise by`,a:[`increasing the exhaust jet velocity.`,`breaking the exhaust into smaller streams and promoting rapid mixing with ambient air.`,`completely blocking the exhaust flow.`],c:1,exp:`These suppressors divide the exhaust into many smaller jets, which mix more quickly with the surrounding air and lower the intensity of the low-frequency noise. The increased mixing shifts noise to higher, less objectionable frequencies.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0054`,q:`The frequency content of jet exhaust noise is significant because`,a:[`high-frequency noise carries farther and is more annoying than low-frequency noise.`,`frequency has no effect on how noise is perceived.`,`low-frequency noise travels farther, so suppressors shift energy to higher frequencies that attenuate more quickly.`],c:2,exp:`Low-frequency sound carries over long distances, so suppressors are designed to convert exhaust noise to higher frequencies that dissipate faster in the atmosphere. This reduces the noise reaching the ground.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0055`,q:`On a reciprocating-engine aircraft, the augmentor tube system can contribute to noise reduction by`,a:[`drawing cooling air through the cowling and helping mix and quiet the exhaust flow.`,`storing exhaust gas to dampen pulses.`,`increasing exhaust back pressure to silence the engine.`],c:0,exp:`Augmentor tubes use exhaust velocity to draw cooling air through the engine compartment and mix it with the exhaust, which can help reduce noise. Their main purpose is cooling, with some noise and thrust benefit.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0056`,q:`A "hush kit" installed on a turbine-powered aircraft is intended to`,a:[`increase the engine's maximum thrust rating.`,`reduce engine exhaust and fan noise to meet noise regulations.`,`decrease fuel consumption during cruise.`],c:1,exp:`A hush kit is a noise-reduction modification that lowers exhaust and fan noise so an older engine can meet stricter noise standards. It typically modifies the exhaust mixing and acoustic treatment.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0057`,q:`Acoustic lining material used in turbofan nacelles reduces noise by`,a:[`reflecting all sound back into the engine core.`,`increasing the fan tip speed.`,`absorbing sound energy from the fan and exhaust ducts.`],c:2,exp:`Sound-absorbing acoustic liners in the inlet and exhaust ducts absorb fan and exhaust noise energy. This treatment is a common part of turbofan noise-reduction design.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0058`,q:`High-bypass turbofan engines are inherently quieter than early turbojets primarily because`,a:[`they discharge exhaust at a lower average velocity.`,`they have no exhaust system at all.`,`they operate at much higher exhaust temperatures.`],c:0,exp:`A large bypass fan moves more air at a lower velocity, reducing the velocity difference between the exhaust and ambient air. This lower jet velocity greatly reduces exhaust mixing noise.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0059`,q:`When inspecting a reciprocating-engine muffler, an important concern besides leaks is`,a:[`the color of the exterior paint.`,`deterioration or collapse of internal baffles that could restrict flow.`,`the brand name stamped on the housing.`],c:1,exp:`Internal baffles can deteriorate, loosen, or collapse, restricting exhaust flow and reducing power, sometimes without external signs. Mufflers must be inspected internally as well as externally.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0060`,q:`Why is a defective muffler on a reciprocating engine a carbon monoxide concern in addition to a noise concern?`,a:[`A muffler produces carbon monoxide that is otherwise absent from exhaust.`,`Mufflers convert oxygen into carbon monoxide.`,`A muffler crack can allow exhaust gas to leak into the heat exchanger air supply.`],c:2,exp:`When the muffler is part of the cabin-heat heat exchanger, a crack can leak exhaust gas, including carbon monoxide, into the cabin air. This makes muffler integrity a safety-of-flight item.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0061`,q:`Noise suppressors are designed to reduce noise while having minimal effect on`,a:[`engine performance and exhaust back pressure.`,`the aircraft paint scheme.`,`the cockpit instrument lighting.`],c:0,exp:`An effective suppressor reduces noise without significantly increasing back pressure or robbing engine performance. Excess restriction would reduce thrust or power, which is undesirable.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0062`,q:`Sound intensity is commonly measured in`,a:[`pounds per square inch.`,`decibels.`,`degrees Fahrenheit.`],c:1,exp:`Sound level or intensity is measured in decibels (dB), a logarithmic scale. Noise standards for aircraft are expressed using decibel-based measurements.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0063`,q:`The corrugated-perimeter (daisy) and multi-tube nozzle designs both reduce jet noise by the common principle of`,a:[`reducing exhaust mass flow to near zero.`,`cooling the exhaust with injected water at all times.`,`increasing the surface area of contact between exhaust and ambient air to speed mixing.`],c:2,exp:`Both designs increase the contact area between the exhaust and the surrounding air, accelerating the mixing process. Faster, finer mixing reduces the strong low-frequency noise of a single large jet.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0064`,q:`Chevrons (saw-tooth patterns) on the trailing edge of some turbofan exhaust nozzles are used to`,a:[`promote gentler mixing of exhaust and bypass air to reduce noise.`,`increase thrust by 50 percent.`,`act as a thrust reverser.`],c:0,exp:`Chevrons encourage smoother, more gradual mixing of the exhaust and bypass streams with ambient air, reducing jet noise. They provide noise benefit with only a small performance penalty.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0065`,q:`When a noise suppressor is added to an engine, the maintenance technician must ensure that it`,a:[`is painted bright orange for visibility.`,`is an approved component for that installation.`,`increases exhaust temperature for efficiency.`],c:1,exp:`Any noise suppressor must be an approved part for the specific installation to ensure it does not adversely affect performance or safety. Unapproved modifications to the exhaust are not permitted.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0066`,q:`One reason exhaust noise is a regulatory concern for aircraft is that`,a:[`it has no effect on communities or operations.`,`noise improves engine cooling.`,`community noise limits restrict operations at many airports.`],c:2,exp:`Aircraft noise affects communities near airports and is subject to regulatory limits, which can restrict operations. Noise suppression hardware helps aircraft comply with these limits.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0067`,q:`A muffler that has developed an internal restriction will most likely cause`,a:[`increased exhaust back pressure and reduced engine performance.`,`an increase in available engine power.`,`lower cylinder head temperatures and more power.`],c:0,exp:`An internal restriction raises exhaust back pressure, which reduces volumetric efficiency and engine power. This can occur from collapsed or shifted internal baffles.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0068`,q:`Compared to a single large exhaust jet, dividing the exhaust into several smaller jets generally produces noise that is`,a:[`louder and lower in frequency.`,`lower in intensity and higher in frequency.`,`unchanged in every respect.`],c:1,exp:`Smaller jets mix faster with ambient air, reducing overall intensity and shifting energy toward higher frequencies that attenuate more rapidly. This is the basic principle of multi-tube suppressors.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0069`,q:`Augmenter tubes on a reciprocating engine installation primarily improve`,a:[`fuel octane rating.`,`propeller blade angle control.`,`engine cooling by inducing additional airflow.`],c:2,exp:`Augmenter tubes use exhaust velocity to draw additional cooling air through the cowling, improving cooling at low airspeeds. They can also reduce noise and add a small amount of thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0070`,q:`When inspecting an exhaust noise suppressor or muffler for airworthiness, the technician should check for`,a:[`cracks, leaks, and internal deterioration.`,`only the manufacturer's logo.`,`the date of the last paint job only.`],c:0,exp:`Suppressors and mufflers must be inspected for cracks, leaks, and internal deterioration that could affect safety or performance. Internal damage may not be visible from the outside.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0071`,q:`An acoustically treated exhaust duct lining must be inspected because damage to the lining can`,a:[`increase the engine thrust beyond limits.`,`reduce its noise-attenuating effectiveness and may shed material.`,`improve fuel economy significantly.`],c:1,exp:`Damaged acoustic lining loses its ability to absorb sound and can shed pieces that may be ingested or cause other damage. Inspection ensures the treatment remains effective and secure.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0072`,q:`The primary trade-off considered when designing a jet noise suppressor is between`,a:[`paint color and corrosion resistance.`,`cockpit ergonomics and seat comfort.`,`noise reduction and engine performance/weight penalties.`],c:2,exp:`Suppressors reduce noise but can add weight and slightly reduce performance, so designers balance noise reduction against these penalties. The goal is effective quieting with minimal performance loss.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0073`,q:`Why does a higher-bypass-ratio turbofan generally meet noise requirements more easily than a low-bypass engine?`,a:[`It accelerates a larger mass of air to a lower velocity, reducing jet noise.`,`It eliminates the combustion process.`,`It runs at a much higher exhaust velocity.`],c:0,exp:`Higher bypass moves a greater mass of air at lower velocity for the same thrust, which lowers the exhaust velocity and the associated mixing noise. This makes high-bypass engines inherently quieter.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0074`,q:`Exhaust gas leaks from a cracked muffler on a single-engine airplane are particularly hazardous because they can`,a:[`increase propeller efficiency.`,`introduce carbon monoxide into the cabin through the heating system.`,`improve exhaust scavenging.`],c:1,exp:`When the muffler also serves as the cabin heat source, a crack can let carbon monoxide into the cabin air. This makes thorough muffler inspection essential for occupant safety.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0075`,q:`Which statement about noise suppression on turbine engines is correct?`,a:[`Noise is best reduced by raising exhaust velocity as high as possible.`,`Noise level has no relationship to exhaust velocity.`,`Noise can be reduced by promoting faster mixing of the exhaust with ambient air.`],c:2,exp:`Encouraging rapid mixing between the high-velocity exhaust and the surrounding air reduces the intensity of low-frequency jet noise. Lowering exhaust velocity through higher bypass also reduces noise.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K3`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0076`,q:`The primary purpose of a thrust reverser system is to`,a:[`redirect engine thrust to help decelerate the aircraft after landing.`,`increase forward thrust during takeoff.`,`improve engine cooling during cruise.`],c:0,exp:`A thrust reverser redirects the engine's gas flow forward to produce a retarding force that helps slow the aircraft after touchdown. This reduces landing roll and brake wear.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0077`,q:`A mechanical-blockage type thrust reverser slows the aircraft by`,a:[`spraying water into the exhaust.`,`placing a movable obstruction such as a clamshell or target door in the exhaust stream to deflect it forward.`,`feathering the compressor blades.`],c:1,exp:`Mechanical-blockage reversers position clamshell or target-type doors in the exhaust path to block and deflect the gas stream forward. The forward-directed flow creates reverse thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0078`,q:`An aerodynamic-blockage (cascade) type thrust reverser produces reverse thrust by`,a:[`closing off the inlet to stop airflow.`,`increasing the fuel flow to the burners.`,`using blocker doors and cascade vanes to turn the fan or gas flow forward.`],c:2,exp:`Cascade reversers use blocker doors to redirect the flow into cascade vanes that turn it forward. This is common on turbofan engines where the fan air provides most of the reverse thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0079`,q:`On a high-bypass turbofan, a cold-stream thrust reverser reverses`,a:[`the cold fan (bypass) air, which provides most of the reverse thrust.`,`only the hot core exhaust gas.`,`the engine oil flow.`],c:0,exp:`Cold-stream reversers act on the bypass (fan) air, which carries most of the engine's airflow on a high-bypass turbofan. Reversing this large mass of cool air provides effective braking.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0080`,q:`The clamshell-type thrust reverser is an example of which category of reverser?`,a:[`Aerodynamic-blockage cascade type.`,`Mechanical-blockage type.`,`Cold-stream fan type.`],c:1,exp:`Clamshell doors physically block and redirect the exhaust stream, making them a mechanical-blockage type reverser. Target-type doors fall into the same category.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0081`,q:`Thrust reverser systems are most commonly actuated by`,a:[`the pilot manually pushing the doors by hand.`,`exhaust gas pressure alone with no controls.`,`pneumatic, hydraulic, or electric power.`],c:2,exp:`Reverser doors and sleeves are moved by pneumatic, hydraulic, or electric actuators controlled from the flight deck. These systems must position and lock the reverser reliably.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0082`,q:`Why are interlocks provided in a thrust reverser system to prevent deployment in flight?`,a:[`Because inadvertent in-flight deployment could cause loss of control.`,`To save fuel during cruise.`,`To increase cruise speed.`],c:0,exp:`Interlocks prevent the reverser from deploying in flight because an inadvertent deployment could create asymmetric or reverse thrust and lead to loss of control. Deployment is normally permitted only on the ground.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0083`,q:`A typical safety interlock allows thrust reverser deployment only when`,a:[`the aircraft is at cruise altitude.`,`ground-sensing logic (such as weight-on-wheels) confirms the aircraft is on the ground.`,`the engine is shut down.`],c:1,exp:`Reverser deployment is normally inhibited unless on-ground conditions, such as weight-on-wheels or other landing logic, are satisfied. This prevents deployment while airborne.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0084`,q:`After the thrust reverser is deployed on landing, the pilot increases engine power to`,a:[`retract the landing gear sooner.`,`raise the exhaust gas temperature for cooling.`,`produce greater reverse thrust for deceleration.`],c:2,exp:`Once the reverser is deployed, advancing the throttles increases the redirected airflow and produces greater reverse thrust to slow the aircraft. Power is then reduced before the reverser is stowed.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0085`,q:`The term "idle reverse" on a thrust reverser system refers to`,a:[`the reverser deployed with the engine at idle power, producing minimal reverse thrust.`,`the reverser stowed during cruise.`,`maximum reverse thrust at full power.`],c:0,exp:`Idle reverse is the condition where the reverser is deployed but engine power remains at idle, producing only minimal reverse thrust. Power is then increased as needed for more deceleration.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0086`,q:`Reverse thrust is usually most effective at`,a:[`low taxi speeds near the end of the landing roll.`,`high speed immediately after touchdown.`,`rotation during takeoff.`],c:1,exp:`Reverse thrust is most effective at the higher speeds just after touchdown, when the redirected airflow produces the greatest retarding force. Its effectiveness decreases as the aircraft slows.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0087`,q:`Why is reverse thrust typically reduced or canceled before the aircraft slows to very low speed on the runway?`,a:[`To increase the landing roll for practice.`,`To raise the cabin pressure.`,`To prevent reingestion of exhaust gases and debris into the engine inlet.`],c:2,exp:`At low speed, reversed exhaust can be drawn back into the engine inlet, causing reingestion of hot gases and debris. Reverse thrust is therefore reduced before reaching very low speeds.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0088`,q:`In a cascade-type thrust reverser, the cascade vanes serve to`,a:[`turn the redirected airflow forward to produce reverse thrust.`,`meter fuel to the engine.`,`cool the turbine blades.`],c:0,exp:`Cascade vanes are curved turning vanes that direct the blocked airflow forward, producing reverse thrust. The blocker doors close the normal flow path so air is forced through the cascades.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0089`,q:`A target-type thrust reverser uses`,a:[`rotating compressor stators.`,`two clamshell-like doors that swing into the exhaust stream to deflect it forward.`,`a water injection nozzle.`],c:1,exp:`A target reverser uses doors that, when deployed, form a target or bucket in the exhaust stream and deflect the gas forward. It is a mechanical-blockage type reverser.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0090`,q:`When a thrust reverser is in the stowed (forward thrust) position, the system should`,a:[`partially block the exhaust to reduce noise.`,`leave the blocker doors halfway deployed.`,`provide an unobstructed gas path for normal forward thrust.`],c:2,exp:`In the stowed position the reverser must provide a clear, unobstructed path so the engine produces normal forward thrust efficiently. Any partial obstruction would degrade performance.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0091`,q:`Maintenance inspection of a thrust reverser system should include checking the actuators and`,a:[`locking and indicating mechanisms for proper operation.`,`the aircraft tire pressure only.`,`the cabin entertainment system.`],c:0,exp:`Reverser maintenance includes verifying that actuators, locks, and position-indicating systems operate correctly. Proper locking and indication are essential to prevent inadvertent deployment.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0092`,q:`Most thrust reverser control systems are arranged so that the reverser can be selected only when the`,a:[`engine is producing maximum forward thrust.`,`throttles (power levers) are at or near idle.`,`engine fire handle is pulled.`],c:1,exp:`The control logic typically permits reverser selection only with the power levers at idle, ensuring the reverser deploys against low airflow. Power is then advanced once the reverser is deployed and locked.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0093`,q:`A thrust reverser position indicating system informs the flight crew that`,a:[`the cabin temperature is correct.`,`the fuel tanks are full.`,`the reverser has reached the deployed or stowed position.`],c:2,exp:`Position indicators tell the crew whether the reverser is deployed or stowed and locked. This feedback is important for safe operation and confirms proper system function.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0094`,q:`On a high-bypass turbofan, reversing only the fan air rather than the core exhaust is effective because`,a:[`the fan air represents the majority of the engine's total airflow and thrust.`,`the core exhaust contains no usable energy.`,`the fan air is colder and therefore heavier than the core gas at all times.`],c:0,exp:`On high-bypass engines the fan air accounts for most of the total airflow and thrust, so reversing it provides substantial braking. This also simplifies the reverser and avoids handling the very hot core gas.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0095`,q:`If a thrust reverser fails to fully stow and lock, the primary concern is that`,a:[`fuel economy will improve.`,`the engine may produce reduced or asymmetric thrust and be unsafe for flight.`,`cabin lighting will dim.`],c:1,exp:`A reverser that is not fully stowed and locked can disrupt normal thrust, create asymmetry, or risk in-flight deployment. Such a condition must be corrected before flight.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0096`,q:`The blocker doors in a cascade thrust reverser function to`,a:[`spray fuel into the bypass duct.`,`open the engine inlet wider.`,`close off the normal fan discharge path so air is forced through the cascade vanes.`],c:2,exp:`Blocker doors close the normal fan exit path when the reverser deploys, forcing the air outward through the cascade vanes. The vanes then turn the flow forward for reverse thrust.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0097`,q:`Thrust reversers reduce wear on the aircraft brakes by`,a:[`providing additional decelerating force during the landing roll.`,`cooling the brakes with bleed air.`,`increasing the landing speed.`],c:0,exp:`By providing a decelerating force, thrust reversers share the braking load and reduce wear on the wheel brakes. They are especially valuable on wet or contaminated runways.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0098`,q:`Which of the following is a typical means of preventing inadvertent thrust reverser deployment in flight?`,a:[`A placard reminding the pilot not to use it.`,`Electrical and mechanical interlocks tied to ground-sensing systems.`,`Painting the reverser controls a bright color.`],c:1,exp:`Reverser systems use electrical and mechanical interlocks tied to landing and ground-sensing logic to prevent in-flight deployment. These safeguards are critical to flight safety.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0099`,q:`During a thrust reverser operational check, the technician verifies that the system deploys, restows, and`,a:[`remains halfway deployed for cooling.`,`disables the engine fire detection system.`,`locks securely in the stowed position with correct indication.`],c:2,exp:`An operational check confirms that the reverser deploys, restows, and locks securely with proper cockpit indication. Reliable stowing and locking are essential for safe forward-thrust operation.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null},{id:`PP12-0100`,q:`Compared with mechanical-blockage reversers, aerodynamic-blockage (cascade) reversers are especially well suited to`,a:[`high-bypass turbofan engines where most thrust is in the fan stream.`,`small reciprocating engines.`,`engines without any bypass air.`],c:0,exp:`Cascade (aerodynamic-blockage) reversers act on the fan bypass air, making them ideal for high-bypass turbofans where the fan provides most of the thrust. Mechanical-blockage types are more common where the hot exhaust must be deflected.`,ref:`FAA-H-8083-32B`,acs:`AM.III.L.K4`,diagram:null}];export{e as questions};